const unit13={
id:13,title:'Trace minerals and iron balance',headline:'Tiny amounts.<br>Essential roles.',intro:'Track iron, zinc, iodine, selenium, copper and other trace elements through absorption, transport, enzymes and clinical context across 12 connected topics.',quizNote:'All 30 supplied source questions are included. The submitted course key is preserved for exam practice. Questions 11, 12, 23 and 30 remain discussion items because their options are ambiguous, context-dependent or reflect claims that have changed. Question 9 uses the course-specific snack answer and explains why it is not a universal food ranking.',
lessons:[
['Trace minerals and regulation','Small requirement does not mean small importance.','<p>Trace minerals are needed in amounts generally below 100 mg per day. They act in enzymes, hormones, oxygen transport, antioxidant defence, growth and tissue maintenance.</p><p>Deficiency and toxicity are uncommon for many trace minerals in well-nourished people, but risk changes with diet, life stage, disease, genetics, supplements and environmental exposure. Most absorbed trace minerals move from the intestine into portal blood, pass through the liver and then circulate bound to proteins or other molecules.</p><h3>Trace-mineral regulation atlas</h3><table><tr><th>Mineral</th><th>Main control point</th><th>Important exit or storage route</th></tr><tr><td>Iron</td><td>Intestinal absorption through hepcidin–ferroportin control</td><td>Stored in ferritin/hemosiderin; no major regulated excretion pathway</td></tr><tr><td>Copper and manganese</td><td>Intestine plus liver handling</td><td>Biliary excretion is especially important</td></tr><tr><td>Iodine and selenium</td><td>Intake, tissue use and kidney handling</td><td>Substantial urinary excretion</td></tr><tr><td>Zinc</td><td>Intestinal absorption and endogenous intestinal losses</td><td>Metallothionein can retain zinc in enterocytes</td></tr></table><div class="example"><b>Sort by regulator</b><p><b>Liver and bile:</b> copper, manganese. <b>Kidneys and urine:</b> iodine, selenium and absorbed chromium. <b>Intestinal gate:</b> iron, zinc and copper.</p></div><div class="example"><b>Soil to body</b><p>Plant mineral content can vary with soil and water. Cooking and processing can change concentration or loss, but regulation after eating is equally important.</p></div>'],
['Metalloenzymes and transport proteins','Put trace minerals into working proteins.','<p>A <b>metalloenzyme</b> contains a metal ion that is required for catalytic activity. Examples include selenium-containing glutathione peroxidases and copper/zinc superoxide dismutase.</p><p>Other proteins move or store minerals: transferrin transports iron, ferritin stores it, ceruloplasmin carries most plasma copper and metallothionein binds metals including zinc.</p><div class="example"><b>Three jobs</b><p>A mineral can be a catalytic cofactor, a structural stabilizer or cargo held by a transport/storage protein.</p></div>'],
['Heme and nonheme iron','Build the meal, then predict absorption.','<p>Heme iron comes mainly from animal tissue and is relatively well absorbed. Nonheme iron occurs in plant foods and fortified foods and is more sensitive to the meal.</p><h3>Nonheme iron decision guide</h3><table><tr><th>If the meal contains…</th><th>Likely effect</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Vitamin C-rich fruit or vegetables</td><td>Absorption increases</td><td>Vitamin C helps keep iron soluble and available</td></tr><tr><td>Meat, poultry or fish</td><td>Absorption increases</td><td>The “meat factor” enhances nonheme iron absorption</td></tr><tr><td>Tea, coffee or concentrated polyphenols with the meal</td><td>Absorption decreases</td><td>Polyphenols can bind or reduce availability of nonheme iron</td></tr><tr><td>High-phytate grains or legumes without an enhancer</td><td>Absorption can decrease</td><td>Phytate can bind mineral ions</td></tr></table><div class="example"><b>Bean-salad upgrade</b><p>Add orange, bell pepper or another vitamin C source. If iron status is low, have tea or coffee between meals rather than with the meal.</p></div><div class="example"><b>Know the course keys—and their limits</b><p><b>Snack comparison:</b> the submitted test keys mushrooms stuffed with bread crumbs. That ranking depends on recipe, serving size and whether the crumbs are enriched, so do not treat it as a universal food hierarchy.</p><p><b>Polyphenols:</b> tea is the course answer, but tea, chocolate, blueberries and tofu can all contain polyphenols.</p><p><b>Coffee or tea range:</b> select <b>40–70%</b> for this course. The real effect varies with beverage strength, polyphenol dose, timing, meal composition and iron status.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iron-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: iron</a></p>'],
['Iron transport, storage and muscle','Follow iron from the enterocyte to its destination.','<h3>The iron journey</h3><ol><li>Nonheme iron enters the intestinal cell after reduction and transport; heme follows a different entry route.</li><li>Inside the enterocyte, iron is either held in <b>ferritin</b> or exported through <b>ferroportin</b>.</li><li>In plasma, <b>transferrin</b> carries iron to bone marrow and other tissues.</li><li>Bone marrow uses iron for hemoglobin; muscle uses iron in <b>myoglobin</b>.</li><li>Excess iron is stored mainly in ferritin and, when stores are high, <b>hemosiderin</b>.</li></ol><table><tr><th>Protein</th><th>Main role</th></tr><tr><td>Hemoglobin</td><td>Carries oxygen in red blood cells</td></tr><tr><td>Myoglobin</td><td>Binds oxygen in muscle</td></tr><tr><td>Transferrin</td><td>Transports iron in plasma</td></tr><tr><td>Ferritin</td><td>Primary intracellular storage protein</td></tr><tr><td>Hemosiderin</td><td>Less readily mobilized storage aggregate</td></tr></table><p><b>Hepcidin</b> from the liver lowers iron entry into plasma by causing ferroportin removal, linking iron status and inflammation with absorption and recycling.</p><div class="example"><b>Memory line</b><p>Transferrin travels; ferritin rests; hemosiderin holds the overflow.</p></div>'],
['Iron deficiency and overload','Recognize risk without diagnosing from one symptom.','<p>Severe iron deficiency produces <b>microcytic, hypochromic anemia</b>: red cells are small and contain less hemoglobin. Infants, menstruating people and pregnant people are at higher risk because needs or losses are greater. An adult man without blood loss is generally at lower risk.</p><p>Iron overload can also cause harm. Hereditary hemochromatosis increases absorption; repeated transfusions and excessive supplements can add risk. Ferritin is useful clinically but also rises with inflammation.</p><div class="example"><b>Do not self-treat blindly</b><p>Anemia has many causes. Iron supplementation should match assessment, especially when symptoms or abnormal blood counts are present.</p></div>'],
['Copper and manganese','Compare two liver-handled trace minerals.','<table><tr><th></th><th>Copper</th><th>Manganese</th></tr><tr><td>Core roles</td><td>Iron metabolism, connective tissue, neurotransmitters and antioxidant enzymes</td><td>Metabolism, antioxidant defence and bone formation</td></tr><tr><td>Useful sources</td><td>Liver, shellfish, nuts and seeds</td><td>Whole grains, nuts, legumes and leafy vegetables</td></tr><tr><td>Main regulation</td><td>Intestinal absorption plus liver and biliary excretion</td><td>Liver handling and biliary excretion</td></tr><tr><td>Deficiency clue</td><td>Rare; can affect blood cells, bone and connective tissue</td><td>Very rare; limited evidence links severe deficiency with poor growth, bone changes and skin rash</td></tr></table><p>Because the liver and bile are important for clearing both copper and manganese, liver disease can impair their regulation. Excess occupational or environmental manganese exposure can be neurotoxic.</p><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Copper-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: copper</a> · <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Manganese-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: manganese</a></p>'],
['Iodine and thyroid function','Follow the feedback loop and separate deficiency from excess.','<div class="example"><b>Thyroid pathway</b><p>Hypothalamus (TRH) → pituitary (TSH) → thyroid iodide uptake and T4/T3 production → negative feedback to pituitary and hypothalamus.</p></div><p>The thyroid takes up iodide under stimulation from <b>thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)</b>. Iodine becomes part of T4 and T3, hormones that regulate metabolism, growth and neurodevelopment.</p><table><tr><th>Too little iodine</th><th>Too much iodine</th></tr><tr><td>Goitre, hypothyroidism, impaired fertility and impaired growth or neurodevelopment; severe population deficiency raises morbidity and mortality</td><td>Can also disturb thyroid function in susceptible people; more is not automatically better</td></tr></table><p>Iodine is largely excreted in urine, so urinary iodine reflects recent population intake better than an individual long-term status from one sample. In the quiz, <b>excessive growth in stature</b> is the exception—not a deficiency outcome.</p><p>Goitrogens in cruciferous vegetables can interfere with iodine use in experimental or high-exposure settings, but ordinary portions are nutritious and usually not a concern when iodine intake is adequate.</p><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iodine-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: iodine</a></p>'],
['Selenium and antioxidant enzymes','Use a narrow essential range with care.','<p>Selenium is incorporated into <b>selenoproteins</b>, including glutathione peroxidases and thyroid-hormone deiodinases. These proteins participate in antioxidant defence and thyroid-hormone metabolism.</p><p>Selenium content in plant foods varies greatly with soil. Brazil nuts can contain very high amounts. Both deficiency and excess are possible; chronic excess may cause hair and nail changes, garlic breath and neurologic symptoms.</p><p>Absorbed selenium metabolites are regulated partly through urinary excretion.</p><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Selenium-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: selenium</a></p>'],
['Chromium: an evidence update','Distinguish historical teaching from current uncertainty.','<p>Older nutrition texts described chromium as essential because animal and early parenteral-nutrition observations appeared to show a diabetic-like deficiency state. The 2001 U.S. Dietary Reference Intake therefore set adequate intakes.</p><p>Current NIH guidance notes that scientists do <b>not currently consider chromium necessary for good health</b>; no clinically defined deficiency has been established in healthy people. Chromium is excreted mainly in urine, and supplement benefits for glucose control are inconsistent.</p><div class="example"><b>How to answer the source</b><p>Chromium is the historical intended answer to the quiz item, but treat the question as discussion rather than a modern fact.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Chromium-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: chromium</a></p>'],
['Molybdenum and manganese pathways','Connect named enzymes with food sources and limits.','<table><tr><th>Mineral</th><th>Enzyme examples</th><th>Food pattern</th><th>Deficiency context</th></tr><tr><td>Molybdenum</td><td>Sulfite oxidase, xanthine oxidase and aldehyde oxidase</td><td>Legumes such as peas, grains and nuts</td><td>Extremely rare outside unusual genetic or medical circumstances</td></tr><tr><td>Manganese</td><td>Manganese superoxide dismutase and enzymes in metabolism/bone formation</td><td>Whole grains, nuts, legumes and leafy vegetables</td><td>Extremely rare; limited evidence links severe deficiency with growth, bone and skin changes</td></tr></table><div class="example"><b>Course wording versus practice</b><p>The source question links molybdenum with liver enzymes used in drug “detoxification.” Select <b>molybdenum; peas, grains and nuts</b> for the course, but understand the precise claim: molybdenum supports specific enzymes, not a general cleanse or supplement effect.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Molybdenum-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: molybdenum</a> · <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Manganese-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: manganese</a></p>'],
['Zinc, metallothionein and inherited malabsorption','Use the mucosal-block idea without oversimplifying iron control.','<p>Zinc supports hundreds of enzymes and transcription factors, immune function, wound healing, taste, growth and reproduction. Animal foods provide readily absorbed zinc; legumes, nuts and whole grains also contribute, though phytate lowers absorption.</p><h3>Two enterocyte binding proteins</h3><table><tr><th>Protein</th><th>Mineral</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Ferritin</td><td>Iron</td><td>Stores iron inside cells; iron retained in a shed enterocyte is not transferred to blood. Hepcidin–ferroportin is the central systemic control.</td></tr><tr><td>Metallothionein</td><td>Zinc</td><td>Binds zinc in enterocytes and can limit transfer to blood when intake is high.</td></tr></table><p>This is the course’s compressed “mucosal block” idea: ferritin and metallothionein can reduce excess entry of iron and zinc, respectively, but they are not identical regulatory systems.</p><div class="example"><b>Infant case</b><p>An infant with growth failure and red, scaly dermatitis around the scalp, eyes, mouth or feet needs clinical assessment. <b>Acrodermatitis enteropathica</b> is an inherited zinc-transport disorder that can cause this pattern and requires treatment.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Zinc-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: zinc</a></p>'],
['Fluoride, arsenic and evidence boundaries','Separate beneficial exposure from essentiality and toxicity.','<p>Fluoride is not generally classified as an essential nutrient, but appropriate exposure strengthens tooth enamel and reduces dental caries; it also influences bone mineral. Excess during tooth development can cause fluorosis.</p><p>Arsenic is a toxic metalloid and human carcinogen in its inorganic forms. Some animal research has explored possible biological roles at extremely low exposure, but arsenic is <b>not established as an essential human nutrient</b>. It should never be framed as a supplement target or approved food additive.</p><div class="example"><b>Final rule</b><p>“Present in biology” does not automatically mean “essential,” and “beneficial at one exposure” does not mean “safe at every dose.”</p></div>']
],
questions:[
{n:1,q:'Liver disease can impair regulation of which trace minerals handled importantly through bile?',o:['Iron and zinc','Selenium and molybdenum','Copper and manganese','Iodine and chromium'],a:2,e:'Copper and manganese homeostasis relies substantially on liver processing and biliary excretion.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:2,q:'Even without genetic disease, supplements or environmental overexposure, deficiency or excess can still occur for:',o:['Iron and iodine','Selenium and molybdenum','Copper and manganese','Zinc and chromium'],a:0,e:'Iron balance varies with losses and life stage, while iodine intake can be too low or high depending on diet and fortification.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:3,q:'Which statement about trace minerals is most accurate?',o:['They occur in only a few foods','All are regulated only by absorption','For most people, deficiencies and toxicities are uncommon','No trace mineral can cause toxicity'],a:2,e:'Most balanced diets provide trace minerals safely, but deficiency and toxicity are possible in specific contexts.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:4,q:'Which is a general characteristic of trace minerals?',o:['Plant content never varies with soil','Cooking never affects mineral content','They circulate from the GI tract to liver and other tissues','Food-source toxicities are common'],a:2,e:'Absorbed trace minerals enter circulation and are distributed through the liver and other tissues; specific routes vary.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:5,q:'Which organ is an important regulation and excretion site for iodine and selenium?',o:['Stomach','Small intestine','Kidneys','Liver'],a:2,e:'Urinary excretion through the kidneys is important for both iodine and selenium balance.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:6,q:'Which pair is substantially excreted in urine?',o:['Iron and iodine','Copper and manganese','Iodine and selenium','Selenium and manganese'],a:2,e:'Iodine and selenium are regulated in part through urinary excretion.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:7,q:'Enzymes that require a metal ion are called:',o:['Zymogens','Trace enzymes','Metalloenzymes','Substrates'],a:2,e:'A metalloenzyme contains a metal required for catalytic function.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:8,q:'What can improve iron absorption from a three-bean salad?',o:['Milk','Orange juice','Tea','Yogurt'],a:1,e:'Vitamin C in orange juice enhances nonheme iron absorption.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:9,q:'The snack item richest in nonheme iron is:',o:['Mushrooms stuffed with bread crumbs','Non-fat yogurt with fruit','Potato chips','Pretzels dipped in peanut butter'],a:0,e:'Course-key answer: mushrooms stuffed with bread crumbs. The result depends on recipe, portion and fortification—especially enriched bread crumbs—so use A for this test rather than treating it as a universal food ranking.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:10,q:'What factor in animal tissue can enhance nonheme iron bioavailability?',o:['Meat factor','Beef factor','Protein factor','Intrinsic factor'],a:0,e:'The “meat factor” describes the enhancing effect of meat, poultry and fish on nonheme iron absorption.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:11,q:'Which listed food contains polyphenols?',o:['Tea','Chocolate','Blueberries','Tofu'],a:0,e:'Tea is the source-intended answer, but all four can contain polyphenolic compounds. Because the item has multiple defensible answers, it is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:12,q:'Consuming one cup of coffee or tea with a meal can decrease nonheme iron absorption by what percentage?',o:['20–50%','30–60%','40–70%','50–80%'],a:2,e:'Course-key answer: 40–70%. Controlled meal studies support inhibition in this general range for some coffee and tea preparations, but there is no universal percentage. Beverage strength, polyphenol dose, timing, meal composition and iron status change the effect, so this item remains unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:13,q:'The two iron storage forms are ferritin and:',o:['Hemoglobin','Transferrin','Albumin','Hemosiderin'],a:3,e:'Ferritin is the main storage protein; hemosiderin is a less readily mobilized storage aggregate.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:14,q:'Which iron-containing protein is found in muscle cells?',o:['Hemoglobin','Myoglobin','Albumin','Hemosiderin'],a:1,e:'Myoglobin binds oxygen in muscle.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:15,q:'Who is least likely to be iron deficient?',o:['A one-year-old','A six-year-old','A pregnant 30-year-old','A 41-year-old man'],a:3,e:'Among these examples, an adult man without described blood loss has the lowest typical iron-deficiency risk.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:16,q:'Severe iron deficiency causes which anemia pattern?',o:['Microcytic, hypochromic','Macrocytic, normochromic','Pernicious','Megaloblastic'],a:0,e:'Iron-deficient red cells are typically smaller and contain less hemoglobin.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:17,q:'Which food is the most concentrated copper source listed?',o:['Liver','Poultry','Broccoli','Winter squash'],a:0,e:'Organ meats, especially liver, are rich in copper.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:18,q:'Copper homeostasis occurs partly through adjustments in:',o:['Sweat loss','Absorption','Urine loss','Ionization'],a:1,e:'Intestinal absorption adjusts with copper status; biliary excretion is also central.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:19,q:'Iodine uptake by the thyroid is regulated chiefly by:',o:['Thyroid-regulating hormone','Thyroid-stimulating hormone','Goitrogen','Thyroxine'],a:1,e:'TSH stimulates thyroid iodide uptake and hormone synthesis.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:20,q:'Goitrogenic compounds that can inhibit thyroid iodine use occur in:',o:['Cruciferous vegetables','Citrus fruits','Red meats','Milk products'],a:0,e:'Crucifers contain glucosinolate-derived compounds, but ordinary intake is generally safe when iodine status is adequate.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:21,q:'Iodine deficiency is not expected to cause:',o:['Severe neurodevelopmental impairment','Excessive growth in stature','Infertility','Higher mortality risk in severe deficiency'],a:1,e:'Iodine deficiency impairs, rather than accelerates, normal growth and neurodevelopment.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:22,q:'Which is a selenoprotein?',o:['Thyroxine','Superoxide dismutase','Cytochrome c oxidase','Glutathione peroxidase'],a:3,e:'Several glutathione peroxidases contain selenium as selenocysteine.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:23,q:'Which mineral was historically designated essential after animal deficiency produced a diabetic-like state?',o:['Chromium','Zinc','Iodine','Magnesium'],a:0,e:'Chromium is the historical intended answer. Current NIH guidance says scientists do not presently consider it necessary for good health, so this item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:24,q:'Excess absorbed chromium is excreted mainly:',o:['Through sweat','Via the lungs','In urine','In feces'],a:2,e:'Absorbed chromium is excreted mainly in urine.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:25,q:'Severe deficiency of which trace mineral can impair growth, skin and bone formation?',o:['Copper','Magnesium','Manganese','Iron'],a:2,e:'Course-key answer: manganese. Human deficiency is extremely rare and signs are not firmly established, but limited evidence associates severe deficiency with poor growth, bone changes and skin rash.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:26,q:'Which pairing supplies a cofactor for enzymes such as sulfite oxidase and aldehyde oxidase?',o:['Molybdenum; peas, grains and nuts','Zinc; milk, meat and seafood','Chromium; fruit, vegetables and grains','Selenium; meat, seafood and nuts'],a:0,e:'Molybdenum is a cofactor in sulfite oxidase, xanthine oxidase and aldehyde oxidase, and legumes, grains and nuts are useful sources. This specific enzyme role should not be generalized into a “detox supplement” claim.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:27,q:'Ferritin and metallothionein are proteins that can:',o:['Promote iron and selenium absorption','Transport iron and chromium','Limit excess entry of iron and zinc, respectively','Deliver iron and copper to liver'],a:2,e:'This is the course’s compressed mucosal-block model: ferritin can retain iron in enterocytes and metallothionein can bind zinc. Systemic iron absorption is controlled chiefly through hepcidin and ferroportin, so the two proteins are not identical regulators.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:28,q:'Which inherited disorder reduces zinc absorption and causes infant dermatitis and growth failure?',o:['Acrodermatitis enteropathica','Hereditary hemochromatosis','Cretinism','Keshan disease'],a:0,e:'Acrodermatitis enteropathica is caused by impaired intestinal zinc transport.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:29,q:'Which nonessential mineral is recognized for strengthening teeth and supporting bone?',o:['Fluoride','Magnesium','Zinc','Molybdenum'],a:0,e:'Fluoride is not generally classified as essential but appropriate exposure reduces dental caries.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:30,q:'Which statement presents arsenic as having a possible biological role?',o:['It controls calcium metabolism','It may act as a cofactor in some experimental systems','Fruit and nuts are recommended arsenic sources','It is an approved food additive'],a:1,e:'B reflects the source wording, but arsenic is not an established essential human nutrient and inorganic arsenic is toxic and carcinogenic. This item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null}
],
cards:[['Trace mineral','Mineral generally needed in less than 100 mg per day.'],['Liver-and-bile pair','Copper and manganese rely importantly on liver handling and biliary excretion.'],['Kidney-and-urine pair','Iodine and selenium are substantially excreted through urine.'],['Metalloenzyme','Enzyme containing a metal required for catalysis.'],['Heme iron','Animal-tissue iron that is relatively well absorbed.'],['Nonheme iron','Plant/fortified-food iron strongly influenced by the meal.'],['Vitamin C and iron','Enhances nonheme iron absorption.'],['Meat factor','Meat, poultry and fish can enhance nonheme iron absorption.'],['Tea and coffee','Polyphenols can inhibit nonheme iron absorption when consumed with meals.'],['Course coffee/tea range','Choose 40–70%; the real effect varies with dose, timing, meal and iron status.'],['Course snack key','Mushrooms stuffed with bread crumbs; actual ranking depends on recipe, portion and fortification.'],['Transferrin','Plasma iron transport protein—the form that travels.'],['Ferritin','Primary intracellular iron storage protein—the form that rests.'],['Hemosiderin','Less readily mobilized iron storage aggregate.'],['Myoglobin','Oxygen-binding protein in muscle.'],['Iron-deficiency anemia','Typically microcytic and hypochromic.'],['Copper source','Liver and other organ meats are concentrated sources.'],['Copper balance','Adjusted through absorption and biliary excretion.'],['TSH','Stimulates thyroid iodide uptake and hormone production.'],['Iodine deficiency','Can impair thyroid function, fertility, growth and neurodevelopment; it does not cause excessive stature.'],['Iodine excess','Can also disturb thyroid function in susceptible people.'],['Iodine excretion','Largely through urine.'],['Selenoprotein','Protein containing selenocysteine, such as glutathione peroxidase.'],['Selenium caution','Both deficiency and excess are possible; soil content varies.'],['Chromium update','Historically labeled essential; current evidence questions essentiality.'],['Chromium excretion','Mainly in urine.'],['Manganese deficiency','Extremely rare; limited evidence links severe deficiency with growth, bone and skin changes.'],['Molybdenum','Cofactor in sulfite, xanthine and aldehyde oxidases.'],['Metallothionein','Binds zinc in enterocytes and can limit transfer to blood.'],['Ferritin versus metallothionein','The course pairs intestinal iron retention with ferritin and zinc retention with metallothionein; hepcidin–ferroportin remains central for iron.'],['Acrodermatitis enteropathica','Inherited zinc malabsorption causing dermatitis and growth failure.'],['Fluoride','Nonessential mineral that strengthens enamel at appropriate exposure.'],['Arsenic','Not an established essential human nutrient; inorganic arsenic is toxic.']]
};
